
<% content_for :title, "What's Your Fucking Normal? | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="baseline-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="baseline-hero">

    <picture class="baseline-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_whats_your_fucking_normal_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_whats_your_fucking_normal_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="baseline-hero-content">

      <p class="baseline-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / KNOW YOUR BASELINE
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>WHAT'S YOUR</span>
        <span>FUCKING</span>
        <span>NORMAL?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="baseline-intro">
        YOU HAVE BIPOLAR DISORDER.
        YOU ALSO HAVE A PERSONALITY.
        LEARN TO RECOGNIZE THE DIFFERENCE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR BASELINE -->
  <section class="baseline-section baseline-pink">
    <div class="baseline-inner">

      <p class="baseline-number">01 / YOUR BASELINE</p>

      <h2>
        START<br>
        WITH YOU.
      </h2>

      <div class="baseline-copy">

        <p>
          What's your fucking normal?
          That's a surprisingly difficult
          question when you have bipolar
          disorder. You're supposed to
          recognize when your mood is
          changing, but how can you know
          what's changing if you haven't
          figured out what you're like
          when you're stable?
        </p>

        <p>
          You're enthusiastic, creative,
          outspoken, impatient, and
          occasionally impulsive.
          You get excited about ideas,
          start projects, and become
          absorbed in whatever happens
          to interest you.
        </p>

        <p>
          None of those things
          automatically means you're
          manic. Sometimes that's
          just who you are.
        </p>

        <p>
          But some of those same
          behaviors can also become
          signs that your mood is
          changing. That's what
          makes knowing your
          baseline complicated.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're not looking for
          behaviors you've never
          experienced before.
          You're looking for familiar
          behaviors that have started
          happening differently.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your baseline isn't some
          imaginary version of
          yourself who's always
          calm, productive, and
          emotionally balanced.
          It's what you're actually
          like when you're
          reasonably stable.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="baseline-callout">
        <p>
          WHO ARE YOU WHEN YOU'RE
          DOING REASONABLY WELL?
          NOT WHO YOU THINK YOU
          SHOULD BE. WHO ARE YOU
          ACTUALLY?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / EVERYDAY PATTERNS -->
  <section class="baseline-section baseline-black">
    <div class="baseline-inner">

      <p class="baseline-number">02 / EVERYDAY PATTERNS</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DOES<br>
        STEADY LOOK<br>
        LIKE FOR YOU?
      </h2>

      <div class="baseline-copy">

        <p>
          You have habits, preferences,
          and personality traits that
          don't disappear when your
          bipolar mood is stable.
        </p>

        <p>
          You like creative projects,
          learning things, spending
          time alone, and following
          interesting ideas.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can be irritated,
          distracted, enthusiastic,
          or spontaneous without
          something being wrong.
        </p>

        <p>
          The useful question isn't
          whether you do these things.
          It's what they ordinarily
          look like for you.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pay attention to how much,
          how often, how intensely,
          and whether you can
          comfortably stop.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="baseline-grid">

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>SLEEP &amp; ROUTINE.</h3>

          <p>
            How much do you normally
            sleep when you're stable?
            What time do you usually
            go to bed and wake up?
            Use a journal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Staying up late once
            isn't automatically
            a problem. Sleeping
            much less than usual
            while feeling unusually
            energetic is something
            worth noticing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>ENERGY &amp; ACTIVITY.</h3>

          <p>
            You naturally enjoy
            working on websites,
            making art, engraving
            glass, and exploring
            new interests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Becoming absorbed in
            a project isn't unusual
            for you.
          </p>

          <p>
            But suddenly starting
            six projects, barely
            sleeping, and feeling
            compelled to keep
            going might be.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>MOOD &amp; IRRITABILITY.</h3>

          <p>
            You're allowed to
            get angry when something
            genuinely pisses you off.
            You're allowed to
            have bad days.
          </p>

          <p>
            But if everything
            suddenly feels unbearable,
            your patience disappears,
            and you're becoming
            unusually reactive,
            consider what's changing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sometimes you're dealing
            with symptoms. Sometimes
            you're simply exhausted,
            in pain, or irritated.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>THOUGHTS &amp; SPEECH.</h3>

          <p>
            You have an active mind.
            ADHD can make your
            attention shift and
            your thoughts jump
            between subjects.
          </p>

          <p>
            That's not automatically
            bipolar mania.
          </p>

          <p>
            But if your thoughts
            become noticeably faster,
            you're talking more
            than usual, and other
            people can't keep up,
            pay attention.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>SPENDING &amp; IMPULSES.</h3>

          <p>
            You like buying materials
            for creative projects
            and finding things
            that interest you.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference is
            whether you're spending
            more frequently, buying
            things you can't afford,
            or convincing yourself
            every new idea requires
            an immediate purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare the behavior
            with your ordinary
            spending patterns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>CONNECTION &amp; ALONE TIME.</h3>

          <p>
            You enjoy being alone.
            That doesn't automatically
            mean you're isolating
            because you're depressed.
          </p>

          <p>
            But there's a difference
            between choosing solitude
            and withdrawing because
            interacting with anyone
            feels exhausting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice when your usual
            preferences start
            looking different.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR RANGE -->
  <section class="baseline-section baseline-yellow">
    <div class="baseline-inner">

      <p class="baseline-number">03 / YOUR RANGE</p>

      <h2>
        YOU'RE A PERSON.<br>
        NOT A FLAT LINE.
      </h2>

      <div class="baseline-copy">

        <p>
          You're allowed to have
          a great day without
          immediately wondering
          whether you're manic.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're allowed to be
          enthusiastic, productive,
          social, and genuinely happy.
          Happiness isn't
          automatically suspicious.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're also allowed
          to be sad, tired,
          disappointed, or frustrated
          without assuming depression
          is returning.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your moods aren't
          supposed to stay
          exactly the same
          every day.
        </p>

        <p>
          Knowing your baseline
          means recognizing the
          difference between
          ordinary variation
          and a meaningful change
          in how you function.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes the difference
          isn't what you're doing.
          It's the intensity,
          frequency, duration,
          and whether you can
          comfortably stop.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="baseline-grid">

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>MY EVERYDAY VARIATION.</h3>

          <p>
            Getting excited about
            something new.
          </p>

          <p>
            Having a productive
            afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feeling irritated
            by an inconvenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wanting a quiet
            day alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Being tired after
            sleeping poorly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Changing your mind,
            starting a project,
            or doing something
            spontaneous.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those experiences
            can all fit within
            your ordinary range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>CHANGES WORTH NOTICING.</h3>

          <p>
            Sleeping much less
            without feeling tired.
          </p>

          <p>
            Starting projects
            faster than you
            can reasonably
            manage them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spending money
            unusually freely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feeling unusually
            confident, restless,
            or irritable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Losing interest
            in things you
            normally enjoy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Withdrawing for
            extended periods
            or struggling with
            ordinary responsibilities.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention when
            several changes
            begin appearing
            together.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="baseline-callout">
        <p>
          YOU CAN BE HAPPY WITHOUT
          BEING MANIC. YOU CAN BE
          MISERABLE WITHOUT BEING
          DEPRESSED. LOOK FOR
          WHAT'S ACTUALLY DIFFERENT.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 / KEEP A RECORD -->
  <section class="baseline-section baseline-black">
    <div class="baseline-inner">

      <p class="baseline-number">04 / KEEP A RECORD</p>

      <h2>
        LEAVE YOURSELF<br>
        SOME<br>
        REFERENCES.
      </h2>

      <div class="baseline-copy">

        <p>
          When you're feeling
          stable, you might
          underestimate how
          difficult your
          depressive episodes were.
        </p>

        <p>
          When you're depressed,
          it can be difficult
          to remember what
          feeling well was like.
        </p>

        <p>
          During mania, you
          might genuinely believe
          you're functioning
          better than ever.
        </p>

        <p>
          Leave yourself something
          to compare those
          experiences against.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to
          turn mood tracking
          into another elaborate
          project. A few
          consistent observations
          are enough to help
          you recognize patterns.
        </p>

       

      </div>

      <div class="baseline-grid">

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>WHAT I WANT TO TRACK.</h3>

          <p>
            Sleep duration
            and changes
            in your routine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mood, energy,
            and irritability.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spending and
            impulsive decisions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Racing thoughts
            or changes in
            how quickly
            you're talking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Interest in creative
            projects and ordinary
            activities.
          </p>

          <p>
            Social withdrawal
            or unusual changes
            in activity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pain, stress,
            and other circumstances
            that might help
            explain the pattern.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>HOW I'LL KEEP IT SIMPLE.</h3>

          <p>
            Use a mood tracker,
            short journal entry,
            or a few notes
            on your phone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Record what matters.
          </p>



          <p>
            Bring useful observations
            to your appointments
            with your psych.
          </p>


        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / YOUR PLAN -->
  <section class="baseline-section baseline-pink">
    <div class="baseline-inner">

      <p class="baseline-number">05 / YOUR PLAN</p>

      <h2>
        KNOW YOURSELF.<br>
        KNOW YOUR<br>
        NEXT MOVE.
      </h2>

      <div class="baseline-copy">

        <p>
          Knowing your baseline
          doesn't accomplish much
          if you recognize
          something is changing
          and then ignore it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes the response
          is simple. Protect
          your sleep, reduce
          unnecessary commitments,
          or pay closer attention
          to your spending.
        </p>

        <p>
          Other times, changes
          are significant enough
          that you need to
          talk with your psych.
          You don't have to
          wait for symptoms
          to become severe
          before reaching out.
        </p>

        <p>
          But don't turn
          self-awareness into
          constant self-scrutiny.
          You already spend
          enough time thinking
          about your thoughts,
          feelings, and behavior.
        </p>

        <p>
          You shouldn't need
          to conduct a psychiatric
          investigation every
          time you're happy,
          tired, or excited.
        </p>

        <p>
          You have bipolar
          disorder. You also
          have an entire
          personality that
          exists independently
          of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Knowing your normal
          means learning to
          recognize both.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="baseline-grid">

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>WHAT HELPS ME STAY STEADY.</h3>

          <p>
            Take your meds consistently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a reasonably
            regular sleep schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to
            changes in spending,
            energy, and
            activity levels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintain sobriety
            and follow your
            established
            recovery plans.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect your
            physical limitations
            and manage pain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make room for
            creativity, connection,
            and rest without
            turning everything
            into an obligation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="baseline-box">
          <h3>WHEN I'LL REACH OUT.</h3>

          <p>
            When you recognize
            several changes
            from your baseline,
            particularly changes
            in sleep, energy,
            judgment, or
            daily functioning.
          </p>

          <p>
            When people you
            trust notice
            significant differences
            in your behavior.
          </p>

          <p>
            When depression,
            agitation, or
            impulsivity is
            becoming harder
            to manage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contact your psych.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="baseline-callout">
        <p>
          YOU'RE NOT LOOKING FOR
          A VERSION OF YOURSELF
          WHO NEVER CHANGES.
          YOU'RE LEARNING WHICH
          CHANGES ACTUALLY MATTER.
        </p>
      </div>
